WEBVTT

1
00:00:13.944 --> 00:00:19.388
Weeks passed and I regained my health, but no mention was made of sending me to school.

2
00:00:20.449 --> 00:00:26.093
Mrs. Reed favored her own children more than ever, and she ordered them not to speak to me.

3
00:00:27.334 --> 00:00:33.379
One day John tried to hit me, but I struck first, punching him in the nose as hard

4
00:00:33.439 --> 00:00:33.940
as I could.

5
00:00:35.092 --> 00:00:38.353
He went blubbering to his mama while I ran upstairs.

6
00:00:39.514 --> 00:00:44.256
I heard him telling a tale of how "that nasty Jane Eyre" had flown at him like a

7
00:00:44.336 --> 00:00:47.697
mad cat, but he was interrupted rather harshly.

8
00:00:47.717 --> 00:00:51.659
"Don't talk to me about her, John," said Mrs.

9
00:00:51.739 --> 00:00:55.060
Reed. "I told you not to go near her.

10
00:00:56.261 --> 00:01:00.062
I don't want either you or your sisters to associate with her."

11
00:01:01.143 --> 00:01:06.985
Leaning over the banister, I cried out, "They're not fit to associate with me!"

12
00:01:08.105 --> 00:01:14.688
Mrs. Reed was a rather stout woman, but on hearing this bold declaration, she ran nimbly upstairs.

13
00:01:15.909 --> 00:01:21.271
She swept me into the nursery and forbade me to rise from my bed or to utter one

14
00:01:21.371 --> 00:01:22.792
more syllable that day.

15
00:01:22.812 --> 00:01:26.834
"What would my uncle say if he were alive?"

16
00:01:27.234 --> 00:01:27.894
I demanded.

17
00:01:29.035 --> 00:01:30.055
"What?" Mrs.

18
00:01:30.135 --> 00:01:31.916
Reed suddenly seemed fearful.

19
00:01:33.064 --> 00:01:38.146
"My uncle Reed is in heaven, and so are Papa and Mama," I went on boldly.

20
00:01:38.166 --> 00:01:41.588
"They can see all you do and think.

21
00:01:42.648 --> 00:01:44.309
They know how you wish me dead."

22
00:01:45.369 --> 00:01:47.390
Mrs. Reed shook me most soundly.

23
00:01:48.470 --> 00:01:54.213
She left me without saying another word, but Bessie lectured me for an hour on what a wicked

24
00:01:54.293 --> 00:01:55.273
child I was.

25
00:01:56.454 --> 00:02:01.656
Christmas and the New Year were celebrated at Gateshead Hall with the usual festive cheer.

26
00:02:02.836 --> 00:02:06.698
Presents were exchanged; dinners and evening parties were given.

27
00:02:07.858 --> 00:02:11.700
Of course I was excluded from every happy moment.

28
00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:17.042
Some evenings when there was company, Bessie would bring me a special treat.

29
00:02:18.823 --> 00:02:24.205
She sat on my bed while I ate, and when I finished, she tucked the covers around me.

30
00:02:25.405 --> 00:02:31.148
Bessie was often quick to anger, but when she was gentle, she was my favorite person.

31
00:02:32.444 --> 00:02:35.026
It was now the morning of January fifteenth.

32
00:02:36.186 --> 00:02:43.610
Eliza was heading outdoors while Georgiana sat in front of the mirror, adorning her hair with artificial flowers

33
00:02:43.670 --> 00:02:44.891
she'd found in the attic.

34
00:02:46.112 --> 00:02:51.955
I was making my bed because Bessie frequently employed me as a sort of nursery maid to tidy

35
00:02:51.995 --> 00:02:52.375
the room.

36
00:02:53.596 --> 00:02:56.898
Having finished my work, I stood in front of the window.

37
00:02:57.999 --> 00:03:02.925
I saw a carriage roll up the drive, but of more interest to me was a hungry robin

38
00:03:03.005 --> 00:03:03.966
perched in a tree.

39
00:03:05.087 --> 00:03:10.129
I crumbled some roll left from my breakfast and was just about to toss it out the window

40
00:03:10.449 --> 00:03:12.791
when Bessie came running into the nursery.

41
00:03:12.811 --> 00:03:15.312
"What are you doing, Miss Jane?"

42
00:03:15.712 --> 00:03:16.953
she cried breathlessly.

43
00:03:16.973 --> 00:03:19.674
"You're wanted in the sitting room."

44
00:03:21.515 --> 00:03:27.519
Bessie quickly scrubbed my hands and face, ran a brush through my hair, and removed my pinafore.

45
00:03:27.539 --> 00:03:29.620
"Who could want me?"

46
00:03:30.832 --> 00:03:33.153
I wondered as I stood outside the sitting room.

47
00:03:34.274 --> 00:03:36.435
Mrs. Reed hadn't summoned me for months.

48
00:03:37.636 --> 00:03:38.616
I entered the room.

49
00:03:39.717 --> 00:03:43.119
Mrs. Reed occupied her usual seat by the fireside.

50
00:03:44.359 --> 00:03:49.502
Standing next to her was a tall, severe man dressed entirely in black.

51
00:03:50.643 --> 00:03:52.103
I curtsied and Mrs.

52
00:03:52.163 --> 00:03:55.905
Reed said, "This is the little girl that I wrote to you about."

53
00:03:57.026 --> 00:04:00.288
The man scrutinized me for some minutes before addressing me.

54
00:04:01.532 --> 00:04:02.733
"Your name, little girl?"

55
00:04:02.753 --> 00:04:04.873
"Jane Eyre, sir."

56
00:04:04.893 --> 00:04:08.355
"Well, Jane Eyre, are you a good child?"

57
00:04:09.555 --> 00:04:11.116
Mrs. Reed answered for me.

58
00:04:11.136 --> 00:04:15.698
"Perhaps the less said on that subject the better, Mr.

59
00:04:15.758 --> 00:04:19.919
Brocklehurst." "I'm sorry indeed to hear that."

60
00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:24.961
Bending from the waist, he installed himself in the armchair opposite Mrs.

61
00:04:25.041 --> 00:04:26.922
Reed. "Come here."

62
00:04:28.023 --> 00:04:31.144
I crossed the rug and he placed me right in front of him.

63
00:04:32.264 --> 00:04:36.525
What a large face he had now that it was almost level with mine!

64
00:04:36.545 --> 00:04:43.867
"There is no sight so sad as a naughty child," he began, "especially a naughty little girl.

65
00:04:44.928 --> 00:04:47.508
Do you know where the wicked go after they die?"

66
00:04:47.528 --> 00:04:51.509
"They go to hell" was my ready and expected answer.

67
00:04:51.529 --> 00:04:54.710
"What must you do to avoid hell?"

68
00:04:55.830 --> 00:05:00.972
I thought for a moment, and when I gave my answer, I knew it was the wrong one.

69
00:05:02.152 --> 00:05:03.093
"I must not die."

70
00:05:04.253 --> 00:05:06.895
Mr. Brocklehurst shook his head sadly.

71
00:05:06.915 --> 00:05:10.037
"I fear that you won't go to heaven."

72
00:05:11.217 --> 00:05:17.601
I looked down at his two large feet planted on the rug and sighed, wishing myself far away.

73
00:05:17.621 --> 00:05:21.823
"Do you say your prayers morning and evening?"

74
00:05:22.203 --> 00:05:22.764
he asked.

75
00:05:22.784 --> 00:05:24.364
"Yes, sir."

76
00:05:24.384 --> 00:05:26.546
"Do you read your Bible?"

77
00:05:26.566 --> 00:05:30.388
"Sometimes." "Are you fond of it?

78
00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:32.901
I hope you like the Psalms."

79
00:05:34.082 --> 00:05:36.284
"Psalms are not interesting," I said.

80
00:05:36.304 --> 00:05:40.907
"That proves you have a wicked heart," concluded Mr.

81
00:05:40.947 --> 00:05:43.209
Brocklehurst. Mrs.

82
00:05:43.289 --> 00:05:47.111
Reed told me to sit down, and then she carried on the conversation.

83
00:05:47.131 --> 00:05:54.397
"Mr. Brocklehurst, I believe I told you in my letter that Jane does not have quite the disposition

84
00:05:54.457 --> 00:05:55.557
that I would wish.

85
00:05:56.718 --> 00:06:01.758
If you admit her to Lowood school, I'd be glad if you requested that the teachers keep an

86
00:06:01.958 --> 00:06:04.500
eye on her because she is deceitful.

87
00:06:05.701 --> 00:06:09.283
I mention this in front of you, Jane, so that you don't try to deceive Mr.

88
00:06:09.323 --> 00:06:15.207
Brocklehurst." She was already wiping hope from my new existence.

89
00:06:16.408 --> 00:06:18.349
I felt tears springing to my eyes.

90
00:06:19.550 --> 00:06:22.992
"Deceit is indeed a sad fault in a child," Mr.

91
00:06:23.032 --> 00:06:23.933
Brocklehurst said.

92
00:06:23.953 --> 00:06:28.476
"It's the same as a falsehood, and all liars go to hell.

93
00:06:29.712 --> 00:06:30.953
I will speak to the teachers."

94
00:06:32.234 --> 00:06:35.556
"I want her to be brought up humbly," continued Mrs.

95
00:06:35.616 --> 00:06:37.237
Reed. Mr.

96
00:06:37.277 --> 00:06:38.318
Brocklehurst nodded.

97
00:06:38.338 --> 00:06:45.143
"My own daughter recently commented on how quiet and plain all the girls at Lowood look with their

98
00:06:45.203 --> 00:06:48.205
hair combed behind their ears and their long pinafores."

99
00:06:49.386 --> 00:06:54.069
Then they settled that I would be transported to Lowood as soon as possible and that I would

100
00:06:54.169 --> 00:06:56.591
also spend all my vacations there.

101
00:06:57.771 --> 00:06:59.244
As he left, Mr.

102
00:06:59.264 --> 00:07:01.325
Brocklehurst handed me a thin book.

103
00:07:03.226 --> 00:07:10.270
"Read it with prayer," he advised, "especially that part containing an account of the awfully sudden death of

104
00:07:10.370 --> 00:07:14.933
Martha G., a naughty child addicted to falsehood and deceit."

105
00:07:16.113 --> 00:07:17.554
He left, and Mrs.

106
00:07:17.614 --> 00:07:19.415
Reed ordered me back to the nursery.

107
00:07:20.536 --> 00:07:23.297
I walked to the door, but then I turned back to Mrs.

108
00:07:23.357 --> 00:07:25.479
Reed. I had to speak.

109
00:07:25.499 --> 00:07:27.720
"I'm not deceitful.

110
00:07:28.852 --> 00:07:33.153
If I were, I'd say I love you, but I declare I don't love you.

111
00:07:34.374 --> 00:07:38.715
As for this book, you should give it to Georgiana because she tells lies.

112
00:07:39.915 --> 00:07:44.916
People think you are a good woman, but you are bad, and I will tell that to everyone

113
00:07:44.976 --> 00:07:45.557
at Lowood."

114
00:07:46.677 --> 00:07:48.037
Mrs. Reed looked frightened.

115
00:07:49.278 --> 00:07:51.018
"What's wrong with you, dear?"

116
00:07:51.038 --> 00:07:53.459
"I am not your dear.

117
00:07:54.659 --> 00:07:56.079
Send me to school soon, Mrs.

118
00:07:56.119 --> 00:07:57.920
Reed, for I hate living here!"
